A||||2||sqoach||Steering wheel not locking||||4||
Z||000000||sqoach||07-28-2001||01:55 PM||||On my 86 SE, my steering wheel does not lock when the key is taken out. The little arrow or line or whatever is matched up to where it says "lock." I'm pretty sure I just need to replace something. Ideas?<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">   <P>84 SE (FOR SALE)*sale pending*<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||4
Z||000001||mrfiero||07-28-2001||02:51 PM||||If you remove the steering wheel you'll see the lockplate.  This is what holds the guts of the column in and is why you need a special tool to press it down to get the snap ring off.<P>Over time cracks/rips develop on the lockplate (it's just plastic).....this causes the lockplate to shift further outward (towards the driver).  There is a ring with cutouts underneath the lockplate where a rod sticks through when the key is removed.  This is what locks your column in one place.  When the lockplate gets damaged and shifts outward it doesn't allow the rod to make sufficient contact with the cutout ring.  My '88 GT has this problem.....a new lockplate will fix it.<P>Another possibility is the rod mechanism is broken on your column.  It is spring loaded and maybe the spring is shot/missing.  This wouldn't allow the rod to pop up when the key is removed.<P>Good luck.      <BR>+++++||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-28-2001||07:39 PM||||info on the column is in my cave with links to pictures of most of it.<P>Either your lock plate is missing <P>Or the more likely the spring on the lock pin has quit. The pin is a royal pain in the a__ to fix.<P>It's remotely posible something deep in the column has failed and the steering wheel and plate have moved toward the driver. This can happen in a straight column. would be almost imposible in a tilt.<P>Lock plate Plastic? Not in any GM column I've worked on. The plate cover is, but that has nothing to do with the actual steel plate or holding it in place. A steel snap ring locks the plate on the shaft. The plastic cover just fills the gap between the wheel and the column.||||reg||1
